INSERT INTO badges (Id, UserId, Name) VALUES (2, NULL, 'Organizer');

-- In this contrived scenario, the join in both Q1 and Q2 will only pick up the first row because the second row does not join with any row in users. 
-- However, if for some reason the query were modified so that the aggregation were computed over rows from badges (for example, by using an outer join), then Q1 (using COUNT(Id)) would count 2 rows while Q2 (using COUNT(UserId)) would count only 1 row, leading to a difference in the computed percentage.

-- Under the strict schema provided, however, no valid counterexample exists because the foreign key and primary key constraints ensure that T1.Id and T1.UserId are never NULL in any row that participates in the join.

